sections.s2_technical:
The underscore `_` is not a real variable name — you cannot READ from it. It's a signal to the compiler and future readers that this binding is deliberately ignored. You can use `_` multiple times in the same scope — unlike regular variables, multiple `_` bindings don't conflict.

In catch blocks, you often catch an exception just to handle the error case but never inspect the exception object. Before Java 22, you had to name it: `catch (IOException e)` where `e` triggers "unused variable" warnings. Now: `catch (IOException _)` — explicit, no warning.

```java
// BEFORE: catch variable 'e' is never used — IDE warning!
try {
    tradeService.settle(trade);
} catch (SettlementException e) {
    log.error("Settlement failed for trade {}", trade.getTradeId());
    trade.setStatus("FAILED");
}

// AFTER: _ = intentionally unused — no warning, clear intent
try {
    tradeService.settle(trade);
} catch (SettlementException _) {
    log.error("Settlement failed for trade {}", trade.getTradeId());
    trade.setStatus("FAILED");
}
```

In enhanced for-loops, sometimes you iterate just to count or trigger side effects, and the loop variable is unused. Before: `for (Trade trade : trades)` with `trade` never used. After: `for (Trade _ : trades)`.

```java
// Count iterations without using the element:
int count = 0;
for (var _ : trades) {
    count++;
    // We don't need the actual trade — just counting
}
```

In lambda parameters, when a BiFunction or BiConsumer has two parameters but you only use one, `_` replaces the unused parameter.

```java
// Map.forEach: we only need the value, not the key
tradesBySymbol.forEach((_, trades) -> {
    log.info("Processing {} trades", trades.size());
});

// Map.merge: we only need newValue, not oldValue
symbolCounts.merge("RELIANCE", 1L, (_, newVal) -> newVal);
```

In record patterns (Java 21+), when destructuring a record and you don't need all components, `_` ignores the unused ones.

```java
record Trade(String tradeId, String symbol, BigDecimal amount, String status) {}

// We only need amount — ignore tradeId, symbol, status:
if (obj instanceof Trade(var _, var _, var amount, var _)) {
    processFee(amount);
}

// In switch:
String classify(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Trade(var _, var _, var amount, var _) when amount.compareTo(limit) > 0
            -> "LARGE TRADE";
        case Trade(var _, var _, var amount, var _)
            -> "NORMAL TRADE";
        default -> "UNKNOWN";
    };
}
```

Multiple `_` bindings in the same scope are allowed — they don't conflict because `_` is never read.

```java
// THREE unnamed bindings in one pattern — all different positions, no conflict:
case Trade(var _, var _, var amount, var _) -> process(amount);
```

